    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> built Tenochtitlan on a small swampy island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, enlarging it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinampa">chinampas</a>, and it became the largest city in the pre-Columbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</li><li>As capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in the 15th century it had causeways, canals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a>, a walled ceremonial centre of about 45 buildings and a population estimated at 200,000 to 400,000.</li><li>Cortés besieged it for over 90 days, disease struck its people, and it fell on 13 August 1521 to the Spanish and Tlaxcaltec; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> was founded on the ruins.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1987 archaeologists found 1,789 human bones five metres below a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> street, a burial of the 1480s at the foot of the main temple of Tenochtitlan.</p></aside>
    </section>

