    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1325</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> found Tenochtitlan on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, by the date now given</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1453</span><span class="rf-text">The levee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> is completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1473</span><span class="rf-text">Tenochtitlan fights a war against its neighbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1486</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a> begins to rule, and the city is rebuilt after a flood</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1519</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> arrives in the city on 8 November</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1521</span><span class="rf-text">The city falls on 13 August to the Spanish and Tlaxcaltec</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1978</span><span class="rf-text">Major excavations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> begin</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexica"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexica.jpg" alt="Mexica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aztec_drums,_Florentine_Codex..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tenochtitlan, also called Mexico-Tenochtitlan, was a Mexican altepetl built on an island in what was then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>. It was one of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> altepetl, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, on the island; the other was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>. The name is traditionally thought to come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> tetl, rock, and nōchtli, prickly pear, and is often read as "Among the prickly pears growing among rocks". One late 16th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, the Bancroft dialogues, suggests the second vowel was short, so the etymology remains uncertain, and it is also thought that the city was named after Tenoch.</p><p>The exact date of the founding is unclear. The date 13 March 1325 was chosen in 1925 to celebrate the 600th anniversary of the city, and the city is dated from 1325. According to the Mexica state religion, the wandering tribes awaited the fulfilment of an ancient prophecy: they would find the destined site for a great city, signalled by an eagle with a snake in its beak perched on a cactus (Opuntia), which had grown from the heart of Copil. The Mexica saw this vision on what was then a small swampy island in the lake. The image is now on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s coat of arms and flag.</p><p>Undeterred by the unfavourable terrain, they set about building their city. They used the chinampa system, misnamed floating gardens, for agriculture and to dry and expand the island, which was perpetually enlarged as the city grew. The city lay on the western side of the shallow Lake Texcoco, the largest of five interconnected lakes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl.jpg" alt="Ahuitzotl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahuitzotl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> came to dominate other peoples around them, and Tenochtitlan grew into the largest and most powerful city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. Commercial routes brought goods from as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> and perhaps even the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca Empire</a>.</p><p>The city's water was managed with large works. During the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a> a levee, called the levee of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nezahualcoyotl">Nezahualcoyotl</a> and reputedly designed by him, was built. It was estimated at 12 to 16 kilometres long and was completed about 1453. It kept fresh spring-fed water around the city and the brackish water of the lake beyond it to the east. Two double <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> of terracotta, each more than 4 kilometres long, brought fresh water from the springs at Chapultepec. After a flood of the lake the city was rebuilt during the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, between 1486 and 1502, in a style that made it one of the grandest in Mesoamerica.</p><p>The city covered an estimated 8 to 13.5 square kilometres. There are no precise figures for its population, which has been estimated at between 200,000 and 400,000, and some popular sources go as high as 350,000. One of the few comprehensive academic surveys of Mesoamerican city sizes reached 212,500 people living on 13.5 square kilometres. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> might have rivalled it among European cities, and it was said to be five times the size of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>. It is also said that Moctezuma ruled an empire of almost five million people in central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, gaining tribute and prisoners to sacrifice to the gods.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii.jpg" alt="Moctezuma II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moctezuma_Mendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tenochtitlan was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a>, founded in 1325, and in the 15th century the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. Its rulers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-i">Moctezuma I</a>, under whom the levee was built; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a>, in whose reign the calendar stone was carved around 1470 and who was the predecessor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tizoc">Tizoc</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a>, who ruled between 1486 and 1502; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a>. The highest social class, the teteuctin, were the rulers of various parts of the empire, including the king.</p><p>The city fought its neighbour <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a> in 1473, a war told in the Anales de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> y Tlatelolco. The two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> are later named together as Tenochtitlan-Tlatelolco.</p><p>The Spanish took the city in 1521. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> founded the Spanish capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on its ruins, and for a time this ciudad de españoles was called Mexico-Tenochtitlan. The Spaniards set up a cabildo, or town council, with jurisdiction over Spanish residents. The outer Indian section, renamed San Juan Tenochtitlan, continued to be governed by the previous indigenous elite and was divided into the same subdivisions as before, and Santiago Tlatelolco likewise had its own municipal council throughout the colonial period. Each kept jurisdiction over settlements on the mainland that supplied labour and tribute demanded by the Spanish, though those settlements gradually gained their own rulers. The city later became a cabecera of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">Viceroyalty of New Spain</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pochteca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pochteca.jpg" alt="Pochteca" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pochteca">Pochteca</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pochtecas_con_su_carga.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city was divided into four zones, each divided into 20 districts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calpulli">calpulli</a>, which were crossed by streets and divided by canals with wooden bridges that were removed at night. Three main streets led to causeways to the mainland at Tepeyac, Iztapalapa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlacopan">Tlacopan</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a> reported them wide enough for ten horses. Bridges in the causeways let canoes pass and could be pulled away to protect the city.</p><p>In the centre, inside a walled square 500 metres to a side, stood about 45 public buildings, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a>, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huitzilopochtli">Huitzilopochtli</a> and the rain god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaloc">Tlaloc</a>, the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quetzalcoatl">Quetzalcoatl</a>, the ball court with its tzompantli, or rack of skulls, and the Sun Temple. Outside it stood the palace of Moctezuma with 100 rooms, each with its own bath, and nearby the cuicalli, or house of the songs, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calmecac">calmecac</a>. The palace held two houses for animals, with about 300 people to care for them, a botanical garden and an aquarium.</p><p>Each calpulli had its own market, and the main market at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlatelolco">Tlatelolco</a>, according to Cortés, was twice the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a> with about 60,000 people trading daily; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernardino-de-sahagun">Bernardino de Sahagún</a> gave a more conservative 20,000 on ordinary days and 40,000 on feast days. Society was highly stratified: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macehualtin">macehualtin</a> commoners, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipiltin">pipiltin</a> nobles, cuauhipiltin eagle nobles, the highest teteuctin, tlacohtin slaves or indentured servants, and the hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pochteca">pochteca</a> merchants, who paid no taxes but sponsored the feast of Xocotl Huetzi. Status showed in houses of reed and mud, adobe brick, or stone masonry.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="Hernán Cortés" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Hern%C3%A1n_Cort%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> arrived in Tenochtitlan on 8 November 1519. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moctezuma-ii">Moctezuma II</a> chose to welcome him as an ambassador rather than risk a war that aggrieved indigenous people might join. Cortés later detained Moctezuma, after an episode in which two Spanish officers sent to Qualpopoca at Nueva Almería were captured and killed, and Moctezuma was supposedly the one who had ordered it. During the festival of Toxcatl, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a>, left in charge, feared a surprise attack, tortured three men until they said it was planned, and had the Spaniards come heavily armed and close every exit of the courtyard.</p><p>Cortés besieged Tenochtitlan for over 90 days, causing a famine. On 13 August 1521, after over two months of fighting, the city fell to the Spanish and their Tlaxcaltec allies, which ended the Aztec empire. Cortés then directed the systematic destruction and levelling of the city, despite opposition, and began its rebuilding.</p><p>The people of the city were also exposed to diseases to which they had no immunity. Symptoms were often delayed for up to ten days, then the infection spread through the body, causing sores, pain and high fever. People were too weak to move or obtain food and water, burying the dead became difficult or impossible, and the death toll rose steadily over the next 60 days. Many native residents died during the siege, though the indigenous population kept a strong presence in the city.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/templo-mayor.jpg" alt="Templo Mayor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Model_of_Tenochtitlan_Ceremonial_Center_Great_Temple_(9755406755).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Todd from Xinzheng, China, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Cortés founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on the ruins, and the site kept symbolic power as the capital of the Aztec empire, which Cortés sought to appropriate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/templo-mayor">Templo Mayor</a> was dismantled and the central district of the Spanish city built over it, and the great temple was destroyed by the Spanish during construction of a cathedral. In the Spanish traza, a European-only zone of 13 blocks in each direction of the central plaza, streets were laid out in a chessboard pattern, while the indigenous districts were irregular and built of modest materials. A royal hospital for indigenous residents was founded.</p><p>The location of the Templo Mayor was rediscovered in the early 20th century, but major excavations only took place from 1978 to 1982, after utility workers came across a stone disc 3.25 metres across showing the dismembered body of the moon goddess Coyolxauhqui. The ruins were built over seven periods, and their weight has made them sink into the lake sediment, so they now rest at an angle. In August 1987 archaeologists found 1,789 human bones five metres below street level, a burial dating to the 1480s at the foot of the main temple.</p><p>Mexico City's Zócalo stands at the site of the original central plaza and market, and many of the old causeways still correspond to modern streets. The calendar stone, 4 metres across and over 18 tonnes, carved about 1470, was found in the ruins. The ruins lie in the historic centre of Mexico City, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> of Xochimilco holds what remains of the geography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> capital: water, boats and floating gardens.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">On the globe, stop by stop</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/journeys?j=cortes">Cortés's March on Tenochtitlan<span>Journey</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Tenochtitlan was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> capital on an island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>, the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> in the 15th century. After a siege of over 90 days and an epidemic it fell to the Spanish and Tlaxcaltec in 1521, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> was founded on its ruins.</em></p>

